kupfer@cip.informatik.uni-wuerzburg.de (Ralf Kupfer) writes:

>: It may be nice, in future versions of Nethack, to allow the player to freely
>: select an alignment, e.g. to play a chaotic Valkyrie or a neutral Knight.

>Agreed. I would like to select the alignment of the character I'm
>going to play...and there's really no reason why a knight has to
>be lawful or an elf to be chaotic, imho.

The alignments are there to balance the game and to put more
distinction between character classes. A knight, for example, is not
only lawful, but he even has the chivalry code that he must stick to.
This would be totally illogical for a chaotic knight. If the alignment
was good/evil, you would have a point, but it's lawful/chaotic. The
original poster mentioned elves, that he couldn't imagine as "evil",
but the elves in Tolkien certainly weren't lawful! I'd sooner call them
chaotic good, chaotic neutral, or even, in some cases, neutral evil.

ANyway, if you let a player choose for himself, there is no reason why
not everyone would choose to be neutral. If you are neutral, you can
become (with a helmet of opposite alignment) both lawful and chaotic,
so you can have ALL the advantages. If you start L/E, you are
restricted to those two, save for converting yourself to neutral.

I think alignments should stay as they are now. They add spice to the
game. You might as well have questions at game start whether you want
the intrinsic gem identify of the archeologist, the automatic curse
detection of the priest, etc. The fact that you cannot choose restricts
you a bit, and that's something that is badly needed in a game like
NetHack where you can do almost everything.

If you really want to play that chaotic knight, you should go through
some trouble to achieve it.

